WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.374
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.430
MORGAN MCLEAN: We're all here
from the OpenCensus community.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:11.490
I work at Google,
Sergey's from Microsoft,

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.180
and Steve is from a stealth
mode startup called Omnition.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.080
We also have a few
people in the audience.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.620
So we have some members of
the OpenCensus team here,

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.463
most notably Bogan Drew,
who's the team mascot.

00:00:22.463 --> 00:00:23.630
And he's sitting over there.

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:25.713
So we'll bring people up
for questions at the end.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.730
So, I'm guessing
that most of you

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.470
already know what OpenCensus is.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.140
For those who don't,
OpenCensus is a set

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.120
of instrumentation libraries--

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.240
a little bit more than
that, but, at its core,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.270
the project is a
set of libraries.

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:43.650
These libraries allow you to
extract distributed traces

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.150
and metrics from
your applications.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.180
They take care of things
like context propagation, tag

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.460
propagation, and
basically all the things

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.290
you need to do to
get observability

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.080
with distributed traces and
metrics out of your app.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.950
OpenCensus is somewhat
unique in the market.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:58.230
It's not just an API.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.880
It's an actual full
implementation.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.800
So we maintain a
single distribution

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:02.730
of these libraries.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.522
There's one for Java,
there's one for Node,

00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:07.680
there's one for C++, and so
on, so that we can ensure all

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.575
of the functionality that
you expect to need is there.

00:01:10.575 --> 00:01:12.700
One of the really interesting
parts with OpenCensus

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.310
is how you get the
data to your back end.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.810
So as I mentioned, it's
just a set of libraries.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.700
We don't do distributed
trace analytics.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.740
We don't do metrics
analytics like projects

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.210
like Prometheus or Jaeger.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.335
We simply allow you to
capture those because that's

00:01:23.335 --> 00:01:24.760
a very difficult problem.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.130
So we have this concept in
OpenCensus called exporters.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.890
Exporter is the way
that you send the data

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.350
from your application
with OpenCensus

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.490
to your back end of choice.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.990
So it's the way you
collect traces--

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.830
it's the way you pass on traces
to something like Jaeger.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:42.770
It's the way you pass on metrics
to something like Prometheus.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.130
So, if we have a sample
microservices app,

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.400
in this case, we have
the front-end service,

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.110
payment service, cart
service, and we're

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.540
calling a database API.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.845
In this case, we have
OpenCensus linked inside

00:01:55.845 --> 00:01:57.470
of our code for each
of these services.

00:01:57.470 --> 00:01:58.857
It provides the APIs we need.

00:01:58.857 --> 00:02:01.190
And it has the instrumentation
that will, in most cases,

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:03.710
automatically capture traces,
do context propagation,

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:05.650
capture critical metrics.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.990
And by linking it there,
we'll capture traces

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.707
from these requests flows
that go through our service.

00:02:11.707 --> 00:02:14.290
And then we'll send this data
to our back end via the exporter

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.895
interface that I
mentioned earlier.

00:02:15.895 --> 00:02:17.770
One of the interesting
things about exporters

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.198
is that you can export to
multiple back ends at once.

00:02:20.198 --> 00:02:22.240
This means you can export
to the tracing back end

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.450
and the metrics back end.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:27.200
It also means that you can
export to multiple tracing

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.982
and metrics back ends
at the same time.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:30.440
This is useful if
you're evaluating

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:32.347
a new vendor or
open-source project

00:02:32.347 --> 00:02:33.680
that you're using as a back end.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.390
It's also useful if you
have different teams who

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:36.350
are using different tools.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:37.725
You can still get
that full stack

00:02:37.725 --> 00:02:41.330
observability with like
Jaeger and Stackdriver,

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.822
for example, or Prometheus
and Stackdriver, for example.

00:02:45.822 --> 00:02:47.780
With that, I'm going to
hand it over to Sergey.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.870
SERGEY KANZHELEV: I'll
seize back for second.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.400
You can count how many languages
this distributed app has.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.500
And, like, four.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.190
And this became a reality.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:05.050
And to build OpenCensus
SDK all these languages

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.310
is a tricky task.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.730
So with OpenCensus,
we're doing that.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:14.350
And I'm happy to tell that for
four languages, Java, Golang,

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:17.980
Node.js, and Python,
we'll announce production

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.380
readiness this month.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.870
For all other languages, it's
coming shortly after that.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.660
So what does it mean
to build OpenCensus

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.930
for a single language?

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:31.510
We want to make sure that
it feels natural for you

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.120
to call with OpenCensus
on your language.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:37.330
The same time, you want to
preserve all the terminology.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:40.120
And also, look and
feel of OpenCensus SDK

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.250
across all the languages
so it's easy for you

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:45.513
to switch back and forth.

00:03:45.513 --> 00:03:46.930
We also strive for
standardization

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:48.260
across languages.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.310
So let's say your distributed
trace goes from one component

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:52.480
to another component.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.850
You shouldn't know that
one component is Golang

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.680
and the other component .NET.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.260
You just know that it will
be correlated magically.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.810
So we'll see telemetry
from both components,

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:06.260
and it will be magically
linked together.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.560
We also strive to
standardize data we collect

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.690
from all those languages.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.140
I mean, it's not--

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.750
it became increasingly
important for you

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.240
to know that you can go
and look at telemetry

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:22.543
from an application code,
of which you don't know,

00:04:22.543 --> 00:04:24.460
because it may be like
your dependent service,

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.020
or service you depend on.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.090
And you are DRI in the
middle of the night,

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:31.810
and you need to look at
telemetry for your sister team

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:32.980
application.

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:35.200
And we strive for
standardization.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.090
I mean, this standardization
is basically,

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.820
you shouldn't worry about
setting a load on a response

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.013
quote for HTTP for
the application

00:04:42.013 --> 00:04:43.180
and language you don't know.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.590
It's a response code for HTTP.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:46.870
You shouldn't care.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:51.940
And we worked on this API
stabilization and testing

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.840
for many languages.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.260
And one thing I want to
talk about is integrations.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:59.050
This OpenCensus SDK--
we want to make sure

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:02.693
that it can express your
application pattern, whatever

00:05:02.693 --> 00:05:03.860
you use in your application.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.770
Like, it may be
simple REST call,

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:09.010
or it may be fancy task
synchronization with a Redis

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:12.420
and database calls, and you can
synchronize it all together.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.940
Or it may be some highly
efficient, queue-based RPC

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:17.380
protocol between your component.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:18.880
And with OpenCensus,
we want to make

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.750
sure you can express all
that in a simple manner.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.550
And to enable that
and to make sure

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:27.070
that you can do all
those integrations,

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:30.860
we created automatic data
collections for technologies.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.990
Like for Java, for instance,
we support HTTP, GRPC, JDBC,

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:38.260
MongoDB, Jetty, Serverlets.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.140
With the stabilization
of APIs and announcement

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.550
of the production rate, we
expect way more integrations.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.360
And integration is
easier to enable--

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:49.660
like, single-gesture way to
enable monitoring and data

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:52.460
collection from
those components.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:56.030
So what's missing
on other languages?

00:05:56.030 --> 00:06:00.600
And-- oh, it's big checkboxes.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:07.940
So on the OpenCensus.io,
we track detailed map

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.910
of all the features
and capabilities

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:11.990
of every single language.

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:13.880
So you can go
check it out there.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.910
Here, I show bird's-eye
view on what's enabled

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.570
and what's supported.

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:21.343
You can see that metrics
API is lagging behind.

00:06:21.343 --> 00:06:23.510
And it's not a surprise
because metrics API was just

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:24.650
introduced recently.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:27.590
We were working on that with
OpenMetrics CNCF project.

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.750
And it just was
introduced recently,

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.370
and we already have
integration for four languages.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:34.988
And if you've been on Morgan's
presentation from last year,

00:06:34.988 --> 00:06:36.780
I want to emphasize
how much progress we've

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.990
made over a year.

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:43.040
I mean, this matrix
was nowhere near that.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.500
And we made the commitment
to make four languages up

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:47.320
to parity, like, four--

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:48.650
all the main features.

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:49.790
And let's use that.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:54.570
So you see all the checkboxes--
four coincide there.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:59.620
So while we're working
on feature parity

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:02.730
on all other languages and
treat it as a high priority,

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:04.310
we also hear feedback
that you need

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:06.300
more signals for observability.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:11.090
And one of the biggest
feedback is you need logging.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:14.480
So we started working
on logging as well.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:17.360
Logs is a very important
observability signal.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:19.370
You need logs to troubleshoot.

00:07:19.370 --> 00:07:22.640
And logs are very easy to
write, easy to collect, easy

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:24.830
to consume, unless it's not.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:26.930
And it's not because
your application

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:31.340
tend to become more complex,
high-scale, and consists

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:35.180
of too many components
written by different vendors.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.110
And this all
affects how you log.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:39.590
With the scale comes the cost.

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:43.130
It became increasingly
costly to enable logs,

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:44.590
so you turn them off.

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:48.920
With complex business
logic, you have hard time

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:52.310
to navigate logs and make sense
of why does this log message

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:53.590
come after that log message?

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:55.160
It doesn't make sense.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.260
And with the increased
number of components

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:00.180
it consume, all the
logs look different,

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:03.110
so you have hard time to
correlate them and understand

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:05.140
what's going on.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:08.560
So we have a plan to
address all those problems.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.610
We will start with a
correlation of logs.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:15.000
And today, you already
have correlation and tags

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:16.878
for distributed traces.

00:08:16.878 --> 00:08:19.170
Pretty soon, you'll be able
to use the same correlation

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:23.980
and tagging to group
and scope your logs.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:27.100
And then we plan
to address some--

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:28.780
our promise is cost.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:31.800
With the collector, you
will be able to do scenarios

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:38.010
like sending verbose logs for
only distributed traces that

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:39.293
had errors in them.

00:08:39.293 --> 00:08:40.710
And you can do it
with retroactive

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:43.770
sampling when you cache
all the informations

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:45.630
and only send subset
of information

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.070
that is most important for
you across all your components

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:52.200
distributed across
multiple languages.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:55.470
Then we want to do scenarios
like a sampling based

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.340
on verbosity of your logs.

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:00.180
You may want to collect only
20% of information logs,

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.073
and errors and
warnings all the time.

00:09:02.073 --> 00:09:03.990
That's the kind of
scenarios we want to enable

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:05.680
for you to address cost issues.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.760
And that can be done
on an OpenCensus Agent

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.010
or even directly in SDK.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:14.520
And then lastly, we will--

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.740
maybe it's a longer
term goal, but we

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:20.820
want to provide better logging
API so you can log consistently

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:22.620
and responsibly.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.010
Consistently means that--

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:25.740
I mean, our mean
bar is very low.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:28.430
Like, you want to make sure
that computer name reported

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:31.660
by one system is the computer
name reported by another system

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:35.100
so you can correlate
those two computer names.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:36.780
What we want ultimately
is to make sure

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:42.900
that we can help you mark up
some log messages as critical,

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.380
like containing password
information, or other PI

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:48.720
information, like personally
identifiable information,

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:50.100
like an IP address.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.340
So we can warn you that
you're doing something strange

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:56.400
and you need to be
very careful with that.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.340
And going forward,
you have more signals.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.560
Like beyond logging,
we have more ideas

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.930
what needs to be
introduced in OpenCensus

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.870
and what needs to
be standardized.

00:10:05.870 --> 00:10:07.885
And I keep saying
standardization

00:10:07.885 --> 00:10:09.260
across languages,
standardization

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:12.220
of signals, standardization
with the new ideas.

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.070
And why standards
are important is

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:17.640
because we want every
library, every application,

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:20.790
and every platform to take
dependency up on OpenCensus

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:23.130
and make sure that the
observability comes out

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:25.650
of the box with every component.

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:27.720
And to do that, we need to--

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.360
this library right is to
trust us, and make sure

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:33.900
that it's a true standard.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:36.540
We have another project in
CNCF doing the same thing,

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.570
doing standardization
across distributed tracing.

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:42.310
It's called OpenTracing.

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:44.940
And I am happy to tell
that we already announced

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:46.420
we're merging projects.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:48.470
This is a big deal.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.040
Everybody was asking about
difference between OpenCensus

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:51.810
and OpenTracing.

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:55.670
There are differences,
but it's better together.

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:58.840
And this merger is
not about one project.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:03.970
And this merger is about merging
community of same-minded people

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:07.060
who want to work together to
build standards for monitoring

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:09.610
and to make sure
that the monitoring--

00:11:09.610 --> 00:11:11.620
you take monitoring
for granted when

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:14.758
you deploy your application.

00:11:14.758 --> 00:11:15.550
It's a full merger.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:17.890
I mean, we'll announce
new name for the project.

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:20.590
And we'll announce [INAUDIBLE]
for all the libraries.

00:11:20.590 --> 00:11:23.860
And all those announcements will
probably come in Kubernetes--

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:28.758
KubeCon conference,
Europe, end of May.

00:11:28.758 --> 00:11:30.300
Another announcement
we want to make,

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:32.242
and as a true
open-source community,

00:11:32.242 --> 00:11:33.950
actually we're working
on standardization

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:36.680
in the external bodies like W3C.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:41.810
And next Monday,
we target declaring

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.540
that the specification of
the trace context going

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:47.108
into a candidate
recommendation stage.

00:11:47.108 --> 00:11:48.650
Candidate recommendation
is the stage

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:52.310
when you can start using this
specification production.

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:57.180
With that, I'd be talking about
data collection standardization

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:58.680
SDKs for a long time.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:01.320
And we're not only making
progress on that front,

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:03.140
we're also making
a lot of progress

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:04.880
on Agent and Collector side.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:08.780
And Morgan will talk
more about that.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:11.630
MORGAN MCLEAN: Thanks, Sergey.

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:14.050
So, we've started talking
the last few months

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:16.270
about some additional
components to OpenCensus.

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:18.070
As I mentioned earlier
in the presentation,

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:19.680
OpenCensus at its
core, and certainly

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:22.180
from the beginning of the
project, is a set of libraries

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:23.960
that you link to
your application.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:25.850
But we have more today.

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:27.970
So starting a few months
ago, we began development

00:12:27.970 --> 00:12:29.540
of the OpenCensus Agent.

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:32.710
This is an application that you
can run on each of your VMs,

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:34.930
or run as a DaemonSet
in Kubernetes,

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:38.020
that will collect traces and
metrics from the OpenCensus

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:38.560
libraries.

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:41.640
So your libraries will export
them to the OpenCensus Agent,

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.390
and which in turn will
then re-export them

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:44.770
to your back end of choice.

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:47.350
One of the big, big benefits
of the OpenCensus Agent

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:48.730
is that you can
choose and change

00:12:48.730 --> 00:12:50.890
your exporters to your
back end without having

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:52.610
to redeploy your app.

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:54.600
This is particularly
big for platforms,

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.770
so certainly inside of Google.

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:58.960
For GKE, if we wanted
to use OpenCensus

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:01.930
to show traces and metrics
that come from the GKE platform

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:03.850
itself, this was a requirement.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.670
We don't have customers rebuild
Kubernetes for themselves.

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:08.920
But it's also useful for
your own applications, right?

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:11.590
It's generally pretty annoying
with OpenCensus, or even

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:13.270
OpenTracing, which
Sergey mentioned.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:17.222
Both of them today require you
to recompile, rebuild, redeploy

00:13:17.222 --> 00:13:18.430
your app to change exporters.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:20.740
With the Agent, you
no longer have to.

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:23.260
The second thing we have is
the OpenCensus Collector.

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:24.640
This is based off of the Agent.

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:26.612
It's, in some sense,
a version of the Agent

00:13:26.612 --> 00:13:28.570
that is running as a
network service, typically

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:30.460
a service inside of Kubernetes.

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:33.983
The Collector will work to take
in all the traces and metrics

00:13:33.983 --> 00:13:34.900
from your application.

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:36.720
They'll be sent
to the Collector.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.908
And because they're all
sent to that central source,

00:13:38.908 --> 00:13:40.450
in addition to
allowing you to change

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:43.750
your exporters in real time
like you can with the Agent,

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:46.060
the Collector will also
allow you to do things

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:47.530
like tail-based sampling.

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:50.020
And so, that means instead
of randomly sampling traces,

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:51.610
you can choose to
just send traces

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:53.650
for high-latency requests,
requests of errors,

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:54.603
to your back end.

00:13:54.603 --> 00:13:56.020
This is a big, big
deal for people

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:57.820
who do distributed
tracing because it means

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:00.657
that the sampling that a
lot of customers do at 1%

00:14:00.657 --> 00:14:01.990
is suddenly much more effective.

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:04.323
And Steve's going to do a
demo of that in a few minutes.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:09.722
Now, cutting to my
laptop, we also, today,

00:14:09.722 --> 00:14:11.930
are showing off a new feature
of the OpenCensus Agent

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:14.360
that we haven't
presented before.

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:17.990
So the OpenCensus Agent,
because it's running on every VM

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:19.970
or is a DaemonSet on
Kubernetes, can also

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:20.990
collect system metrics.

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:23.490
This is something we just added
right before the conference,

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:25.320
but it's now there
in the source code.

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:28.880
And, ah, it went to sleep.

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:31.258
And so, what we've got
here is Stackdriver.

00:14:31.258 --> 00:14:32.300
This could be Prometheus.

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:33.758
This could be any
metrics back end.

00:14:33.758 --> 00:14:35.690
It would effectively
be the same.

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:37.640
And we've got the
OpenCensus Agent

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:41.300
running on a number of our VMs
for the hipster shop demo app.

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:45.140
And we're collecting CPU time
for the system CPU time, user

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:48.877
CPU time, idle time,
and information

00:14:48.877 --> 00:14:50.960
about all the processes
running on those machines.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:52.840
This is coming from
the OpenCensus Agent.

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:55.675
It's collected directly
from that machine--

00:14:55.675 --> 00:14:57.050
or from those
machines, actually.

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:58.430
There's multiple.

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:00.302
This is a big, big
vision into where

00:15:00.302 --> 00:15:02.510
we're going to be in a few
months with the OpenCensus

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:03.010
Agent.

00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:05.870
Our goal, or our
plan, is to also

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:08.600
have it be a great place
to collect all the system

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:11.060
metrics that you need from
things like collectd, statsd,

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:12.170
and so on.

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:14.750
And so by deploying
this OpenCensus Agent

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:18.560
to each of your VMs by pushing
out the OpenCensus libraries

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:20.150
to each of your
applications, you

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:23.120
can now get application
telemetry and system

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:24.740
telemetry that are
correlated and sent

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:26.390
to your back end of choice.

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:28.405
This is pretty easy to deploy.

00:15:28.405 --> 00:15:30.530
I'm not going to go into
depth, but what I can show

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:32.940
you here is the
exporter configuration.

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:35.030
So this is inside
one of the services

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:38.120
that already has the OpenCensus
library linked to it.

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:40.490
You simply configure the
OpenCensus Agent exporter,

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:42.630
point it at the agent.

00:15:42.630 --> 00:15:46.010
And then we have a YAML file
that deploys the OpenCensus

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:47.420
Agent as a DaemonSet.

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:49.070
It's really, really simple.

00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:50.450
The images are
already pre-baked,

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:52.117
so you can just use
them, and it unlocks

00:15:52.117 --> 00:15:54.750
a ton of powerful functionality
that we haven't had before.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:57.710
We'll switch back to the slides.

00:15:57.710 --> 00:15:59.550
And with that, I'm
going to give you Steve.

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:02.008
He's going to give you a great
demo of the OpenCensus Agent

00:16:02.008 --> 00:16:03.840
and Collector.

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:05.150
STEVE FLANDERS: Thanks, Morgan.

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:07.160
All right, so we've talked
a lot about OpenCensus.

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:08.410
There's different pieces here.

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:11.020
We have the client libraries,
we have the OpenCensus Service.

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:12.770
So now, I actually
want to demonstrate how

00:16:12.770 --> 00:16:14.420
you can leverage this power.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:16.700
Let's start with the
client libraries.

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:19.490
So first, let's assume I want
to instrument an application.

00:16:19.490 --> 00:16:21.695
I'm going to use a
Java application here.

00:16:21.695 --> 00:16:24.320
And I know it's early, so we'll
walk through this line by line.

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:26.340
You don't have to
try to figure it out.

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:28.670
But how would you go ahead
and add instrumentation

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:31.010
to your code so you can take
advantage of, let's say,

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.120
distributed tracing
in this case?

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:35.720
So first, you need to
instantiate a tracer.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:37.970
Basically, this allows
you to manipulate and add

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:41.090
additional metadata about
the types of interactions

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:44.990
between your microservices, or
even your function-level calls.

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:47.583
Next, you need to export
this to a back end.

00:16:47.583 --> 00:16:49.250
It doesn't matter
what back end that is.

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:50.510
Here, I'm using Zipkin.

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:52.258
It can be any
exporter destination.

00:16:52.258 --> 00:16:54.050
I'll talk about the
ones that are supported

00:16:54.050 --> 00:16:57.210
by OpenCensus in a little bit.

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:59.460
Next, you determine
your sampling policy.

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:02.910
By default, OpenCensus
samples 1 in 10,000 traces.

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:06.240
If you want to do something less
aggressive, you can do that.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:09.780
In this case, for demo purposes,
I'm sampling everything.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:11.707
Clearly, at scale, that
may not make sense.

00:17:11.707 --> 00:17:13.290
But for the purpose
of demonstrations,

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:15.119
that's pretty straightforward.

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:17.609
Those three lines are
really the only things

00:17:17.609 --> 00:17:19.349
that you need to
configure globally

00:17:19.349 --> 00:17:20.623
for each of your languages.

00:17:20.623 --> 00:17:23.040
So in the case of Java here,
I can go ahead and throw this

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.170
into my common library
and then reuse it

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:27.450
across my application stack.

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:30.210
Then all I really need to do
is add the instrumentation

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.100
to the calls that I'm
making between my services.

00:17:33.100 --> 00:17:35.140
Now for demo purposes,
there is a sleep in here.

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:37.330
That's because there's
actually batching being done.

00:17:37.330 --> 00:17:38.310
And if you don't
wait long enough,

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:40.075
you wouldn't actually
see the traces.

00:17:40.075 --> 00:17:40.950
That's why it's here.

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:43.320
But in your application, you
clearly wouldn't have that.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:46.200
And then finally, here is
how you get a trace, right?

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:49.350
Here's the root span for
example, of a call coming in.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:52.512
You can do the exact same thing
to create a child span as well.

00:17:52.512 --> 00:17:53.970
So for each of your
function calls,

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:56.340
or at least between your
microservices calls,

00:17:56.340 --> 00:17:58.080
you can go ahead
add instrumentation,

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.485
and that can help you
stitch together a trace.

00:18:00.485 --> 00:18:01.860
It's really that
easy, all right?

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:03.730
There's not much to it.

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:05.902
In addition, you can
enhance these logs,

00:18:05.902 --> 00:18:07.860
or enhance these traces,
with additional pieces

00:18:07.860 --> 00:18:09.563
of metadata like logs.

00:18:09.563 --> 00:18:10.980
So if you wanted
to-- for example,

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:14.370
if there was an error stack
maybe of a Java stack trace

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:17.033
that you want to include, you
can embed that into the span.

00:18:17.033 --> 00:18:18.450
When you're creating
the span, you

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:20.520
can say if there is an
error, go ahead and tack

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:23.080
on that log message as well.

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:26.170
You can also add rich
metadata like key-value pairs.

00:18:26.170 --> 00:18:28.898
So, you could add things
like, what region is

00:18:28.898 --> 00:18:29.940
this service deployed in?

00:18:29.940 --> 00:18:32.490
What version of the
code is it running?

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:35.753
And you can use this metadata to
actually get some rich insights

00:18:35.753 --> 00:18:36.420
out of the data.

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.960
And I'll show that in a second.

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:42.000
So that's it from a client
library perspective.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:43.620
Clearly it would be different
if you're using Go or Node

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:45.620
or what have you, but
there's good documentation

00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:48.035
we have an OpenCensus.io
to help get you started.

00:18:48.035 --> 00:18:49.910
If you already have your
own instrumentation,

00:18:49.910 --> 00:18:52.820
that's fine as well, because
the OpenCensus Service actually

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:55.610
supports a wide variety
of formats today.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:58.040
So as Morgan was talking
about, the OpenCensus Service

00:18:58.040 --> 00:19:00.560
is really made up of two
different components, the OC

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:02.970
Agent and the OC Collector.

00:19:02.970 --> 00:19:05.823
And you can choose to use
one, either, both, or neither.

00:19:05.823 --> 00:19:06.740
It's really up to you.

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:09.150
It comes down to your
business requirements.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:12.050
So here, I'm showing
some different topologies

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:13.640
of how you might deploy this.

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:16.760
The OC Agent can actually be
deployed with your application

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:17.810
as a binary.

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:20.185
It can be deployed as a
sidecar if you're using Docker.

00:19:20.185 --> 00:19:21.560
It can be deployed
as a DaemonSet

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:23.330
if you're in the
Kubernetes world.

00:19:23.330 --> 00:19:25.190
The Collector is a
standalone application

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:28.130
and can handle aggregation
of this data as well.

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:30.240
And of course, you have
the OC client library

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:31.770
which you can use as well.

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:34.422
Now, it doesn't matter how
you mix and mingle this.

00:19:34.422 --> 00:19:36.380
Like, for example, as
Morgan was talking about,

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:37.670
you have different back ends.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:40.050
Here, I have Zipkin,
Prometheus, and Omnition.

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:41.850
All right, well, maybe I don't
want to use Zipkin anymore.

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:43.183
I want to switch over to Jaeger.

00:19:43.183 --> 00:19:44.840
It's just a
configuration change.

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:47.360
Everything else is
handled automatically.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:50.030
Maybe I don't want to use the
OC library because I already

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:51.410
have instrumented in Jaeger.

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:52.220
No problem.

00:19:52.220 --> 00:19:55.580
Jaeger is accepted by the Agent
and the Collector as well.

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.560
Let's say that I don't
want to use the Agent.

00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:58.880
I just want to send
directly to the Collector.

00:19:58.880 --> 00:19:59.540
That's fine.

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:00.440
I don't want to
use the Collector.

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:01.815
I only want to
send to the Agent.

00:20:01.815 --> 00:20:02.430
That's fine.

00:20:02.430 --> 00:20:04.130
But at the end of
the day, we would

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:06.380
recommend you leveraging
all the different components

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:08.600
because they provide
different value adds.

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:11.330
But you can choose to piece
this together however it makes

00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:13.500
sense for your environment.

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:15.110
Now, some of the
values provided here

00:20:15.110 --> 00:20:18.200
with the OpenCensus Service
you now only have to support

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:20.310
a single exporter destination.

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:22.910
So for example, if I'm using
the OC client libraries,

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:25.155
I can export and just
go, and I'm all set.

00:20:25.155 --> 00:20:27.530
I don't have to worry about
writing that in Java and Node

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:28.667
in different languages.

00:20:28.667 --> 00:20:29.750
That makes it much easier.

00:20:29.750 --> 00:20:31.970
One thing to maintain,
one exporter.

00:20:31.970 --> 00:20:33.380
That's really nice.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:36.200
In addition, I can change
my back end whenever I want.

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:39.140
After I've instrumented and I've
sent to the OpenCensus Service,

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.180
it's a configuration
change to send my data

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:42.198
to a different location.

00:20:42.198 --> 00:20:43.990
Whether I want to
replicate it so I send it

00:20:43.990 --> 00:20:46.790
to two different back ends or
I want to switch back ends,

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:49.160
all that translation is
handled automatically for you

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:50.840
in the OpenCensus Service.

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:52.340
And the Collector
actually offers

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:54.530
some advanced
functionality, for example,

00:20:54.530 --> 00:20:56.960
intelligent or tail-based
sampling-- the only

00:20:56.960 --> 00:20:59.870
open-source solution today that
actually has that built in.

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:02.037
Redaction support-- you can
actually strip away tags

00:21:02.037 --> 00:21:04.495
if, for whatever reason, there's
information there that you

00:21:04.495 --> 00:21:05.630
don't want to be sharing.

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:07.490
You can tack on
additional metadata,

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:08.867
like region information.

00:21:08.867 --> 00:21:11.450
Maybe you don't want to add that
into your application itself.

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:13.283
You could actually do
it from the Collector,

00:21:13.283 --> 00:21:14.930
and actually say
all spans that are

00:21:14.930 --> 00:21:16.670
being received
from this Collector

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:18.135
should have this region tag.

00:21:18.135 --> 00:21:20.090
So there's a lot of
flexibility here depending

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:24.150
on the requirements that
you're trying to meet.

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:25.980
And really, the
OpenCensus Service

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:28.270
is about universal support.

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:30.600
So here are just a few
of the different types

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:33.270
of receivers and exporters
that are supported today--

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:34.710
a wide range.

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:37.312
And this OpenCensus Service
is extremely flexible.

00:21:37.312 --> 00:21:38.770
You can actually
add more, and more

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:40.360
are being added all the time.

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:42.960
So it doesn't matter whether
you don't see your logo up here.

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:44.457
It can be added.

00:21:44.457 --> 00:21:46.290
And you'll be able to
transform into and out

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:47.910
of these different formats.

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:50.440
So receivers are how you
get data into the system,

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:52.590
so either into the Agent
or into the Collector.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:55.120
Exporters are how you get
data out of the system.

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:57.690
So all this translation is
handled for you automatically,

00:21:57.690 --> 00:22:01.590
making it a truly
vendor-agnostic solution.

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:03.510
Now, one thing you
might be thinking about

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:05.580
is, OK, let's say I'm
using the Collector.

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:06.600
What about scale?

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:08.770
Does this thing actually scale?

00:22:08.770 --> 00:22:09.550
The answer is yes.

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:11.050
We actually have
performance numbers

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:12.930
on the OpenCensus
Service GitHub page

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:14.150
that you can take a look at.

00:22:14.150 --> 00:22:17.370
But as an example, we
have been testing it

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:20.670
at significant scale, for
example, 17.3 billion spans

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:21.480
a second.

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:26.580
That's equivalent to about
17.3 billion spans a day,

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:29.820
which is equivalent to about
200,000 spans a second.

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:33.640
That roughly accumulates
to 57 terabytes of data.

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:35.970
These were 3.3-kilobyte spans.

00:22:35.970 --> 00:22:37.890
Span sizes can
vary anywhere from,

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:42.263
like, say, 100 to 200 bytes
up to maybe 10, 11 kilobytes,

00:22:42.263 --> 00:22:44.430
depending on the number of
tags or the rich metadata

00:22:44.430 --> 00:22:46.990
that you've added
there, with no drops.

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:49.000
And this was on a
single collector.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.960
So typically, we're seeing
about 10,000 spans per second

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:55.020
for 1.2 cores of compute.

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:56.530
And this does scale linearly.

00:22:56.530 --> 00:23:00.722
So you can make it as small
or as large as you want.

00:23:00.722 --> 00:23:02.930
Another reason why you might
consider the Collector--

00:23:02.930 --> 00:23:04.490
I talked a little bit
about intelligent sampling,

00:23:04.490 --> 00:23:06.020
and Morgan mentioned it as well.

00:23:06.020 --> 00:23:09.650
So there is a notorious problem,
if you will, with tracing,

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:11.810
and that is that it's
extremely verbose.

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:13.970
This is very similar
to logging, but there's

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:16.710
no verbosity level in tracing.

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:19.203
So you have a couple of options
on how to deal with this.

00:23:19.203 --> 00:23:20.870
You can either try
to collect all of it.

00:23:20.870 --> 00:23:22.730
At scale, that can be very hard.

00:23:22.730 --> 00:23:24.860
Head-based sampling
is very common.

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:27.773
Basically when you're about to
create the initial root span,

00:23:27.773 --> 00:23:29.690
you make a sampling
decision on whether or not

00:23:29.690 --> 00:23:32.430
you want to actually
sample that request or not.

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:33.930
The problem with
head-based sampling

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:35.513
is that you don't
actually know what's

00:23:35.513 --> 00:23:36.770
going to happen downstream.

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:39.720
You're making the decision
at the entry point.

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:41.900
So for example, maybe
the root span is fine,

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:43.850
but there was an
error down the stack.

00:23:43.850 --> 00:23:47.250
You won't know if you didn't
actually sample that trace.

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:49.370
Tail-based sampling takes
the opposite approach.

00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:51.890
It basically says, let me
collect the entire trace,

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:53.870
and then make a
sampling decision.

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:55.910
So you can actually do
some pretty cool things.

00:23:55.910 --> 00:23:58.400
You can say, I only want
to sample error traces.

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:01.760
If any of the spans contain
an error, let's sample that.

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:03.080
You can do tag-based.

00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:05.492
So you can look for certain
metadata in these spans

00:24:05.492 --> 00:24:07.700
or in these traces, and then
make a sampling decision

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:08.525
based on that.

00:24:08.525 --> 00:24:10.400
Or my personal favorite,
you can do something

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:11.540
like retrieval-based.

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:14.370
So you can actually say, let's
make a sampling decision.

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:17.210
Let's assume that a
trace was not sampled.

00:24:17.210 --> 00:24:19.490
And then your back end
can say, hey, actually

00:24:19.490 --> 00:24:20.540
I do need that trace.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:21.500
Do you still have it?

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:23.360
And I can actually ask
the collector for it

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:24.943
and pull it out and
actually make sure

00:24:24.943 --> 00:24:26.990
that it does get ingested,
or ensure that it's

00:24:26.990 --> 00:24:28.640
ingested going forward.

00:24:28.640 --> 00:24:30.500
We actually have a demo
of this, so you can

00:24:30.500 --> 00:24:31.730
go ahead and take a look at.

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:33.980
It's all available
on the GitHub page.

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:35.540
So it's actually
a Docker Compose.

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:37.040
And I want to walk
through that real

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:40.130
quick with a demo, which I hope
will show up well on the screen

00:24:40.130 --> 00:24:41.160
here.

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:42.372
Let's take a-- oop.

00:24:42.372 --> 00:24:43.216
AUDIENCE: Nope.

00:24:43.216 --> 00:24:43.985
STEVE FLANDERS: Do we switch?

00:24:43.985 --> 00:24:45.770
AUDIENCE: It's still
part of the slide there.

00:24:45.770 --> 00:24:46.640
STEVE FLANDERS: It's
part of the slide.

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:47.140
OK, yeah.

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:49.140
Can we switch back?

00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:51.480
Perfect.

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:54.530
And let's try.

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:55.370
All right.

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:56.525
Actually, it looks pretty
good on a big screen.

00:24:56.525 --> 00:24:57.450
I like this.

00:24:57.450 --> 00:24:59.930
So, here is the
tail-based sampling demo.

00:24:59.930 --> 00:25:01.940
Basically, it's a
Docker Compose file.

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:04.220
It's made up of a
configuration file

00:25:04.220 --> 00:25:05.960
to deploy the Docker containers.

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:08.520
Here, there are going to be
two different Jaeger instances.

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:09.895
And then it has
the configuration

00:25:09.895 --> 00:25:11.300
for the OpenCensus Service.

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:14.270
So we're basically configuring
two different destinations,

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.730
one to receive
only error traces,

00:25:16.730 --> 00:25:20.040
and the other to receive both
error and non-error traces.

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:21.560
So we can go ahead
and fire this up.

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:23.310
You can run this
locally on your system.

00:25:23.310 --> 00:25:25.070
You can deploy this into
your public cloud of choice.

00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:26.150
It doesn't matter.

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:27.740
It will spin up a
Cassandra instance

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:30.800
with different key spaces
for the two Jaeger instances.

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:32.300
It will fire up Jaeger.

00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:33.800
And then in the
OpenCensus world,

00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:35.585
we use this hipster demo shop.

00:25:35.585 --> 00:25:37.460
And there's actually a
load generator utility

00:25:37.460 --> 00:25:40.610
that will go ahead and send in
traces from the hipster demo

00:25:40.610 --> 00:25:43.592
shop and send that into the
OpenCensus Service, which

00:25:43.592 --> 00:25:45.800
will then handle the splitting
of the traffic for you

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:46.980
automatically.

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:49.140
It takes a few seconds
to get spun up here.

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:52.580
And then you can go ahead and
navigate to 17,000 and 18,000.

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:54.630
That's the two different
Jaeger instances.

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:57.590
So, the first one receives all
of the traces, all of the spans

00:25:57.590 --> 00:25:59.010
that are being received.

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:01.250
So here, we can take a
look at that cart service.

00:26:01.250 --> 00:26:03.800
And you can see that
there are spans coming in,

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:05.640
but there are no
errors up there.

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:07.670
And if we look at
18,000, and again we

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:09.290
go look at the cart
service, you'll

00:26:09.290 --> 00:26:11.600
see that we only
see error traces.

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:14.210
That's because we have
tail-based sampling enabled,

00:26:14.210 --> 00:26:17.270
and we basically said we want
only traces that contain errors

00:26:17.270 --> 00:26:19.490
to go to one destination,
we want everything

00:26:19.490 --> 00:26:20.750
to go to the other.

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:22.610
It's very simple,
very easy to configure

00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:24.761
with a YAML-based configuration.

00:26:28.060 --> 00:26:29.560
All right, one more quick demo.

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:31.460
So let's tie this all together.

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:33.370
We have our client
libraries-- pretty easy

00:26:33.370 --> 00:26:34.780
to get instrumented and started.

00:26:34.780 --> 00:26:36.920
If you already have your
existing instrumentation,

00:26:36.920 --> 00:26:38.020
you can leverage that.

00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:39.880
We talked about the
OpenCensus Service

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:42.370
and how it's easy to get
data in and change it

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:45.250
into different formats so you
can switch different back ends

00:26:45.250 --> 00:26:47.710
or take advantage of more
advanced functionality

00:26:47.710 --> 00:26:49.540
such as tail-based sampling.

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:51.580
But what is really
the power of the data

00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:53.860
that you get on the other end?

00:26:53.860 --> 00:26:56.530
Now, OpenCensus is more than
just distributed tracing.

00:26:56.530 --> 00:26:57.490
It's also metrics.

00:26:57.490 --> 00:26:58.718
It's also logs.

00:26:58.718 --> 00:27:00.760
But I'm going to show an
example with distributed

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:03.460
tracing because that's very
powerful in the cloud-native

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:04.300
world.

00:27:04.300 --> 00:27:06.790
With microservice-based
architectures,

00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:09.040
it's really critical to
understand how your system is

00:27:09.040 --> 00:27:12.760
behaving, and so just looking
at individual symptoms

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:14.390
is typically not sufficient.

00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:16.270
You want context
and correlation,

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:19.137
and that's what distributed
tracing can provide for you.

00:27:19.137 --> 00:27:21.470
So we've talked a little bit
about the hipster demo app.

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:23.020
If you haven't seen
it before, here's

00:27:23.020 --> 00:27:25.540
kind of a visual
representation of it.

00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:28.900
Every rectangle here
represents a different service.

00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:30.977
It's actually a
polyglot architecture.

00:27:30.977 --> 00:27:32.560
So every one of these
boxes is written

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:33.737
in a different language.

00:27:33.737 --> 00:27:36.070
This is available on GitHub,
so you can actually take it

00:27:36.070 --> 00:27:38.020
for a spin and try it out.

00:27:38.020 --> 00:27:39.880
And basically, we
want to demonstrate

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:43.197
what a request flow through
this system might look like.

00:27:43.197 --> 00:27:45.280
So if you've ever used a
distributed tracing tool,

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:47.620
let's say Zipkin
or Jaeger, then you

00:27:47.620 --> 00:27:50.350
may be familiar with
something similar to this.

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:52.670
It's just an actual trace.

00:27:52.670 --> 00:27:55.150
So you actually see
the different calls

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:56.760
through a single request here.

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:58.510
Here, you can see
coming in the front end,

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:00.850
hitting the checkout
service, payment service,

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:02.300
a few other services.

00:28:02.300 --> 00:28:04.990
So now I know the calls that
were made for certain requests

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:06.440
into my system.

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:09.160
I also get some information
about the duration of how long

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:10.930
those different operations took.

00:28:10.930 --> 00:28:13.600
That can be useful from
a performance standpoint.

00:28:13.600 --> 00:28:16.467
And for each of these, as
they're known as spans,

00:28:16.467 --> 00:28:17.800
you actually have rich metadata.

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:20.860
So you can drill into it
and you can get information

00:28:20.860 --> 00:28:22.720
about what that call did.

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:25.360
So in the case of,
like, RPC or REST calls,

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:27.790
you might get the status code.

00:28:27.790 --> 00:28:30.190
I talked about tacking on
metadata to the collector.

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:33.075
So here, for example, you
can see region information.

00:28:33.075 --> 00:28:35.200
Maybe you have build
information about your version

00:28:35.200 --> 00:28:37.000
that you can tack on as well.

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:40.180
You can control how
much rich information

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:42.760
you want to include
in these spans.

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:45.168
But you can also do some
more interesting things

00:28:45.168 --> 00:28:46.210
with distributed tracing.

00:28:46.210 --> 00:28:50.260
And for that, I want
to show one more demo.

00:28:50.260 --> 00:28:51.480
Yeah, all right.

00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:52.440
So let's start here.

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:53.850
I'll pause it first.

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:57.050
So here is the hipster
demo app again.

00:28:57.050 --> 00:29:00.420
This is built purely
from distributed traces.

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:03.350
So if you're receiving
traces from Zipkin or Jaeger

00:29:03.350 --> 00:29:06.260
or the OpenCensus client
library, just distributed

00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:09.140
tracing information, you can go
ahead and construct the hipster

00:29:09.140 --> 00:29:10.550
demo shop.

00:29:10.550 --> 00:29:12.620
And this is what it
would look like visually.

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:14.930
And you can see the different
calls that are happening.

00:29:14.930 --> 00:29:16.847
And if there are errors
or problems with them,

00:29:16.847 --> 00:29:17.962
they clearly stand out.

00:29:17.962 --> 00:29:19.920
You can also get information
about the back end

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:21.890
databases or any other
platform services

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:23.180
that you're leveraging.

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:25.880
Now for this particular
example, let's assume

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:28.010
that you're hearing that
the recommendation service

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:29.930
and production is
having problems,

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:32.670
and you're being asked to
go ahead and investigate it.

00:29:32.670 --> 00:29:34.400
So let's go ahead and
do that using only

00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:36.500
distributed tracing information.

00:29:36.500 --> 00:29:39.140
So I can go ahead and actually
click on the recommendation

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:39.812
service.

00:29:39.812 --> 00:29:41.270
And I can get some
rich information

00:29:41.270 --> 00:29:43.320
about the requests that
are being made here.

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:44.720
I see a lot of red.

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:46.490
That's probably bad.

00:29:46.490 --> 00:29:48.180
But there's high
cardinality in my data.

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:49.190
Let me go ahead
and break it down

00:29:49.190 --> 00:29:50.990
by the different
environments I have.

00:29:50.990 --> 00:29:53.870
Well, now I can see that, yes,
only production has errors.

00:29:53.870 --> 00:29:55.790
Stag and development is fine.

00:29:55.790 --> 00:29:57.680
I can see that for
my different regions,

00:29:57.680 --> 00:29:59.060
they're both receiving errors.

00:29:59.060 --> 00:30:00.680
That doesn't give
me much information.

00:30:00.680 --> 00:30:02.055
But I can also
see that a certain

00:30:02.055 --> 00:30:04.100
build number, build 235--

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:06.780
that is the one that
is experiencing issues.

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:09.440
So very quickly, I can go and
say, I have a bad build here.

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:11.000
I need to go roll that back.

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:13.070
It's impacting my environment.

00:30:13.070 --> 00:30:15.260
That's all with just
distributed tracing data,

00:30:15.260 --> 00:30:17.060
not with metrics, not with logs.

00:30:17.060 --> 00:30:19.250
That provides some rich
correlation and context

00:30:19.250 --> 00:30:21.693
in a rich way to actually
divide my data up.

00:30:21.693 --> 00:30:23.360
And I can actually
take this distributed

00:30:23.360 --> 00:30:25.520
tracing information, and
let's say with the logging

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:27.170
spec that Sergey
was talking about,

00:30:27.170 --> 00:30:31.040
you can tack on information like
the trace ID and the span ID.

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:33.740
And now I can actually
say, for a given trace,

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:37.040
show me all the logs for that
request, or from a given log,

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:39.290
go ahead and show me the
topology of how that actually

00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:41.750
looks from a call
perspective in my environment

00:30:41.750 --> 00:30:43.160
or my application.

00:30:43.160 --> 00:30:44.420
It's very powerful.

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:46.610
And all this is enabled
through OpenCensus.

00:30:52.790 --> 00:30:54.560
All right, so, call to action.

00:30:54.560 --> 00:30:56.510
No presentation would
be complete without one.

00:30:56.510 --> 00:30:57.680
Please join us.

00:30:57.680 --> 00:30:59.990
There is a lot to do in
the OpenCensus community.

00:30:59.990 --> 00:31:01.700
There are plenty
of client libraries

00:31:01.700 --> 00:31:04.670
that we would love your help
on the OpenCensus Service.

00:31:04.670 --> 00:31:06.692
We have several
GitHub issues open.

00:31:06.692 --> 00:31:08.150
They're usually
pretty well labeled

00:31:08.150 --> 00:31:09.830
with "help wanted" or
"good first issue,"

00:31:09.830 --> 00:31:11.600
if you're interested
in contributing back.

00:31:11.600 --> 00:31:13.367
Please go ahead and
join us on Gitter.

00:31:13.367 --> 00:31:15.200
We have a Gitter community
where we actually

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:17.150
talk about OpenCensus publicly.

00:31:17.150 --> 00:31:18.920
And if you're
interested in trying

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:20.390
more of the
intelligent sampling,

00:31:20.390 --> 00:31:21.900
that's available as well.

00:31:21.900 --> 00:31:24.530
All that is on GitHub.

00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:26.818
And with that, I'll turn
it back over to Morgan.

